Subject: Re: Switching from SVN/R-Forge to git/github
In-Reply-To: <526D2E21.8030304-B71PBEe7S7Y@public.gmane.org>
References: <526C29A9.2090806@yorku.ca> <CAFXiavb69QV0XaMJfKU4Sg+QD6S-XbmhAiP9zQOYPTFkVTB5iw@mail.gmail.com> <526D2E21.8030304@yorku.ca>
Message-ID: <526E26F3.3080000@walware.de>

Hi Michael,

Michael Friendly wrote [2013-10-27 16:15]:
> On 10/27/2013 4:28 AM, Erdal Karaca wrote:
>> There are command line tools to convert svn to git:
>> https://help.github.com/articles/importing-from-subversion
>> AFAIK, EGit has no such functionality.
> OK, once I install Egit, and use something like svn2git to convert, then
> push the new git local
> repo to github, how can I work with it in StatET?  I think I need to
> create a separate project
> in my eclipse workspace; is that right?

After the conversion using svn2git, you can (for example) in 
Eclipse/StatET switch to the 'Git' perspective and in the
'Git Repositories' view:
  - Add your local repo, so you can easily manage it using EGit
  - Change the branch using 'Switch To' in the context menu,
    if required
  - Select the 'Working Directory' or a subfolder which, you want
    to use as Eclipse project, and start 'Import Projects...' from
    the context menu. It allows you to import existing project (if the
    .project file is still present) or also to setup a new project
    (so you can configure it as R project).

Regards,
Stephan


